.TH GPU-ARBITER 8 "June 2026" "gpu-arbiter" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
gpu\-arbiter \- gaming\-first GPU arbiter daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpu\-arbiter
.RB [ \-\-config | \-c
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-check\-config ]
.br
.B gpu\-arbiter status
.RB [ \-\-config | \-c
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-url
.IR URL ]
.RB [ \-\-json | \-q | \-\-quiet ]
.br
.B gpu\-arbiter wait
.RB [ \-\-for
.IR available | claimed ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR SECS ]
.RB [ \-\-url
.IR URL ]
.br
.B gpu\-arbiter watch
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-url
.IR URL ]
.br
.B gpu\-arbiter
.RB [ \-\-version | \-V ]
.RB [ \-\-help | \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gpu\-arbiter
is a Linux root daemon for a workstation that is a
.B gaming PC first, AI workstation second.
It detects game launches and instantly evicts the configured GPU compute tenants
(Ollama by default, plus any managed unit) from the GPU so the game has the card
to itself, then restores those tenants when gaming ends. Tenants are driven
through systemd by default, or through per\-unit command overrides on hosts
without systemd (see
.BR gpu-arbiter-config (5)).
An HTTP
.B /status
endpoint lets other machines tell whether the box is currently free for AI work.
.PP
Run with no subcommand, the daemon takes
.B no required arguments
beyond the optional config\-path flags below. It reads all of its settings from a
TOML config file (see
.BR gpu-arbiter-config (5) );
a missing file yields built\-in defaults, so the daemon is usable with zero
configuration. The
.B status
subcommand and the
.B \-\-check\-config
flag are additive helpers that do not change the existing systemd\-unit\-driven
deployment.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-config ", " \-c " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to the TOML config file. Precedence, highest first:
.BR \-\-config / \-c ,
then the
.B GPU_ARBITER_CONFIG
environment variable, then the default
.IR /etc/gpu-arbiter/config.toml .
A missing file is not an error \(em the daemon falls back to built\-in defaults.
Applies both to the daemon and to the
.BR status / wait / watch
clients (which read it only for the port, and only when
.B \-\-url
and
.B GPU_ARBITER_URL
are both unset \(em see DAEMON LOCATION). See
.BR gpu-arbiter-config (5).
.TP
.BI "\-\-url " URL
.RI ( status / wait / watch
only) explicit daemon base URL, e.g.\&
.IR http://host:48750 .
See DAEMON LOCATION.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Load and validate the resolved config, print
.BI "OK: " <path>
(or the typed validation error) and exit 0 on success / 1 on error, without
starting the daemon. Cross\-platform \(em works on the non\-Linux stub build.
An unknown or typo'd key at any level
.RI ( top\-level ", " [[managed_units]] ", or " [[game_patterns]] )
fails validation naming the offending key, rather than silently defaulting.
.TP
.BR status " [" \-\-json " | " \-q | \-\-quiet ]
Query a running daemon: GET
.B /status
and print a human\-readable summary.
.B \-\-json
prints the raw
.I /status
JSON instead.
.B \-q
/
.B \-\-quiet
prints nothing; the exit code alone reports state \(em see EXIT STATUS.
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-quiet
are mutually exclusive. A plain HTTP client (no TLS, via
.BR ureq ),
so it runs on any host that can reach the daemon \(em including the non\-Linux
stub build. See DAEMON LOCATION below for how the target is resolved.
.TP
.BR wait " [" \-\-for " " available | claimed "] [" \-\-timeout
.IR SECS ]
Poll
.B /status
every 2 seconds until the daemon reaches the requested state, then exit 0.
.B \-\-for
defaults to
.BR available ;
.B claimed
maps to the daemon's
.B gaming
wire state (the wire vocabulary is gaming/available today; a later release may
rename it, but the
.B wait
interface itself will not change).
.B \-\-timeout
defaults to 60 seconds; on timeout, or if the daemon is unreachable, exits 1
with a message on stderr. Intended to replace a hand\-rolled poll loop in a
launch script, e.g.\&
.BR "gpu-arbiter wait --for available --timeout 30 && ./start-inference.sh" .
.TP
.BR watch " [" \-\-json ]
Poll
.B /status
every 2 seconds and print one line per state transition (plus the initial
observation) to standard output, until killed. The default line is
human\-readable
.RI ( timestamp "  " old " -> " new "  claims=" ... );
.B \-\-json
emits one compact JSON object per line (NDJSON) instead:
.BR "{\(dqts\(dq:...,\(dqfrom\(dq:...,\(dqto\(dq:...,\(dqclaims\(dq:[...]}" .
This is a client\-side polling loop, not a server\-side streaming endpoint
\(em the daemon's HTTP surface stays GET\-only for reads. A transient fetch
error is logged to stderr and polling continues; standard output stays a pure
transition log, safe to pipe.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the program name and version (the
.B CARGO_PKG_VERSION
baked from the git tag at release build time) to standard output and exit 0.
Handled before any runtime setup, so it works even on the non\-Linux stub build.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a short usage summary \(em including the config path and the available
subcommands \(em to standard output and exit 0.
.SH DAEMON LOCATION
The
.BR status ", " wait ", and " watch
clients resolve the daemon to query with the following precedence, highest
first:
.IP 1. 4
.BI "\-\-url " URL
.IP 2. 4
the
.B GPU_ARBITER_URL
environment variable (matching
.BR gpu-arbiter-tray (1)'s
convention)
.IP 3. 4
.I http://127.0.0.1:<port>
using the
.B port
from the local config (resolved as in
.BR \-\-config " above)"
.PP
Only the third form reads a local config file \(em a caller using
.B \-\-url
or
.B GPU_ARBITER_URL
to reach a remote daemon never needs one to exist.
.SH DETECTION AND EVICTION MODEL
Control is
.B level\-triggered reconciliation
(the Kubernetes controller pattern): a reconcile pass observes ground truth (a
.I /proc
scan plus, optionally, the GPU's compute processes), recomputes the set of game
"claims", and drives the managed units accordingly. State is never
delta\-maintained, so it self\-heals across crashes, restarts, and dropped events.
.PP
Reconcile passes are driven by three sources:
.TP
.B cn_proc events
The kernel process\-event connector (netlink) reports fork/exec/exit. A launch
fires a debounced reconcile within milliseconds. Locally launched and
Moonlight\-streamed games are both just local processes, so both are detected.
.TP
.B A periodic backstop timer
A slow interval (default 30 s) reconciles regardless, covering any dropped
netlink events.
.TP
.B Startup
A synchronous reconcile runs before anything else can drive the managed units,
so a daemon restart or host boot never starts a GPU tenant into a live game.
.PP
Detection rules: every Steam game runs under
.I "reaper SteamLaunch AppId=<id>"
and yields a
.I steam:<appid>
claim (zero config, covers all Steam games). Non\-Steam launchers are matched by
configured cmdline substrings
.RI ( game_patterns ).
.PP
Every eviction
.B stops
the unit \(em a game launch, a busier tenant, the gaming invariant, a manual
stop. On a game launch every unit below
.B game_priority
is stopped, in the configured order. "Evicted" means
.BR "not running" ,
the one postcondition observable on every platform. Each eviction runs the
same bounded loop: stop the unit via its supervisor
.RB ( systemctl " stop, or the per-unit " stop_cmd " override),"
then poll the GPU backend
.RB ( nvidia-smi " or the " amdgpu " sysfs reader, per " gpu_backend )
until VRAM\-used drops below the free threshold (graceful release) or the
eviction timeout elapses. On timeout, if the unit is already inactive its VRAM
is already released and the release is treated as graceful; otherwise the unit
is force\-killed via its supervisor
.RB ( "systemctl kill -s SIGKILL" ", or the per-unit " kill_cmd )
and the daemon proceeds regardless \(em gaming wins the GPU unconditionally and a
game launch must never hang waiting on a tenant. If any managed unit fails to
evict, the daemon still hands the GPU to gaming, but marks the state
.B degraded
until the next clean eviction pass or a clean return to
.IR available ,
so an operator can tell a wedged tenant apart from a fully\-reclaimed GPU.
On Windows the wait is for the service to reach STOPPED, and
.B eviction_timeout_s
(default 5) is what bounds it before
.B kill_cmd
runs. A
.B stop_cmd
that fails outright with the unit still up is escalated to the kill as well,
and still counted as an error. On every platform the postcondition is
.BR "not running" :
a VRAM drop only counts once the unit is confirmed stopped, and a unit still
running (or unconfirmed) 2 s after the kill is an eviction error, whatever the
kill command's exit status.
.PP
While gaming, every steady pass re\-checks those units and stops any
that is running again (a service manager restart, a start from outside the
daemon), with a warning in the log at most once a minute per unit. An
unknown run state counts as running. After a stop that needed the kill or
failed, the unit is backed off (15 s, doubling to 120 s, reset when gaming
ends) so a misbehaving unit cannot make every pass a full kill cycle.
.B gaming
ends only once no game has been seen for
.B game_exit_grace_s
seconds in a row (default 30, at most 3600;
.B 0
ends it on the first clean pass); until then nothing is resumed or started and
.I state
stays
.IR gaming .
Each eviction is logged at info level with the unit, its reason, the outcome,
and total VRAM before and after.
.PP
.B yield_cmd
and both
.B yield_timeout_s
keys are deprecated since 0.14.0: they still parse, so an older config keeps
loading, but they do nothing, and the daemon warns about them once at startup.
.B resume_cmd
runs once after the daemon brings a stopped unit back (and once per unit at
startup) and must be idempotent; use it to clear tenant\-side park flags. It
runs only once the unit is up (started by that pass, or confirmed running).
The pass re\-scans processes immediately before each start and each resume;
the first game it sees skips every remaining start and resume in that pass,
and a unit whose resume was skipped stays owed.
.B busy_cmd
is not run for a held unit or one confirmed stopped.
.PP
Tenants also rank against
.I each other
on a priority ladder: a demand at priority
.I P
preempts every unit with
.IR "priority < P," and a game claims at
.B game_priority
(default 100), above every tenant's default (50). Inter\-tenant preemption does
.B not
move
.I state
to
.IR gaming / evicting
\(em those are reserved for a real game holding the GPU \(em and is visible
through
.I units[].running
instead. See
.BR gpu-arbiter-config (5).
.SH HTTP CONTROL SURFACE
The read\-only surface is a single TCP port (default 48750, bind address
.BR bind ,
default 127.0.0.1 \(em loopback only). Set
.B bind
to a LAN address to let other hosts read
.IR /status " and " /metrics ,
and firewall the port yourself if you do. The
.B write
path \(em
.I /units/*
\(em is platform\-specific. On
.B Linux
it is a
.B unix control socket only
.RB ( socket_path ,
default
.IR /run/gpu-arbiter/gpu-arbiter.sock ,
mode 0600 root\-owned, inside a mode 0700 root\-owned parent directory) \(em
local\-root\-only, no bearer tokens. On
.B Windows
there is no unix\-socket listener, so the write routes are served on the same
TCP port instead, restricted to
.B loopback peers only
\(em see PLATFORMS below. Both transports validate
.I {unit}
against
.B managed_units
before touching
.BR systemctl .
See
.BR gpu-arbiter-config (5)
for
.B bind
/
.BR socket_path .
.TP
.B GET /status
Full state snapshot: overall state
.RI ( gaming " | " available " | " evicting ),
active claims, a per\-managed\-unit array (a tristate
.B running
flag \(em true/false/null, the last meaning the daemon couldn't confirm either
way \(em reported
.B models[]
list plus a
.B models_error
string when that query failed, attributed VRAM, and whether the unit is manually
.BR held ),
total GPU memory, and a top\-level
.B degraded
flag (see DETECTION AND EVICTION MODEL).
.TP
.B GET /metrics
Prometheus text\-format exposition. The per\-unit eviction series
.RB ( gpu_arbiter_evictions_total
for every outcome,
.B gpu_arbiter_eviction_duration_seconds
for every stage) are pre\-seeded at 0 for each configured unit at startup, so
they exist before the first eviction. The full metric list is in
.IR docs/reference.md .
.TP
.B GET /healthz
Liveness probe.
.TP
.B "POST /units/{unit}/start, /units/{unit}/stop"
Manual override for debugging \(em on the unix socket on Linux, or on this
TCP port (loopback peers only) on Windows.
.I {unit}
must be one of the configured managed units; an unknown unit is rejected with
404. Routes through the same reconcile task that drives automatic
eviction/restart (never a directly\-racing HTTP handler).
.B stop
stops the unit like any eviction and additionally
.B holds
it down: without the hold, the ensure\-running self\-heal step would
restart it on the very next reconcile pass (even the periodic backstop
timer), making a manual stop a self\-reverting no\-op. A held unit stays down
across game launches/exits until
.B start
(which clears the hold) or a daemon restart \(em holds are in\-memory only.
.B start
is
.B rejected with 409 Conflict
while a game holds the GPU (state
.IR gaming " or " evicting )
\(em a unit started mid\-game would only be stopped again by the next pass's
gaming invariant. On rejection nothing
changes: the unit is not started and any hold stays in place \(em retry once
the state is
.IR available .
It is likewise rejected with 409 while a busy higher\-priority tenant
preempts the unit (the same pass would stop it again); the hold stays.
.B start
on a held, stopped unit starts it, clears the hold, and runs the
.B resume_cmd
the stop left owed in the same pass.
.B start
on a unit that is already running (started outside the daemon, or one whose
stop failed) skips
.BR start_cmd ,
and clears the hold; any owed
.B resume_cmd
then runs once, as soon as the GPU is free for the unit. It replies 200. A failed start
replies 500 and keeps the hold; the daemon log carries the command's exit code
and bounded stderr and stdout.
Example, on the unix socket:
.RS
.nf
curl \-\-unix\-socket /run/gpu\-arbiter/gpu\-arbiter.sock \e
  \-X POST http://localhost/units/ollama.service/stop
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/gpu-arbiter/config.toml
Daemon configuration. Missing file \(rA built\-in defaults. See
.BR gpu-arbiter-config (5).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Tracing/log filter (e.g.\&
.IR info ", " debug ).
Defaults to
.I info
when unset.
.TP
.B GPU_ARBITER_URL
Daemon base URL for
.BR status / wait / watch
(and
.BR gpu-arbiter-tray (1)),
overriding the local\-config\-derived default. See DAEMON LOCATION. Lower
precedence than
.BR \-\-url .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Any command line rejected by the parser (unknown flag, missing/malformed
value, an invalid flag/subcommand combination) exits
.B 2
immediately, before doing any work \(em true for every subcommand.
.TP
.B 0
Normal: informational flag handled,
.B enabled = false
in config (the daemon logs and exits cleanly),
.B \-\-check\-config
on a valid config,
.B status
printed successfully (or, with
.BR \-q / \-\-quiet ,
state is
.IR available ),
or
.B wait
reached its target state.
.TP
.B 1
Run as the daemon on a non\-Linux host (it requires the cn_proc netlink
connector and
.IR /proc ,
plus a GPU backend \(em
.B nvidia-smi
or the
.B amdgpu
sysfs reader \(em and a unit supervisor:
.B systemctl
or the per\-unit
.B *_cmd
overrides). Also returned by
.B \-\-check\-config
on an invalid config; by
.B status
(without
.BR \-q )
on a fetch/render error or an unreachable daemon; by
.B status " " \-q
when the state is
.I gaming
or
.IR evicting ;
and by
.B wait
on timeout or an unreachable daemon.
.TP
.B 2
.RB ( status " " \-q " only)"
the daemon is unreachable \(em a distinct code from the general
.B status
fetch\-error code (1) so a script can tell "GPU claimed" from "can't even ask".
.PP
SIGTERM (systemd stop) and SIGINT (Ctrl\-C) trigger a real graceful shutdown:
the reconcile task is asked to stop picking up new triggers, but any pass
already in flight \(em including an eviction's stop \(-> poll\-VRAM \(->
SIGKILL window \(em always runs to completion first, bounded by a 15\-second
join timeout. Only the stateless I/O tasks are terminated immediately.
.PP
SIGHUP logs a notice that config is not hot\-reloaded and a restart is
required \(em it does not reload, reset, or terminate the daemon. This is
safe: startup always reconciles against observed truth before anything can
touch a managed unit, so a restart never "loses" state.
.SH PLATFORMS
The daemon runs on
.B Linux
and
.BR "Windows 11" .
The reconciliation core is shared; only observation and unit control differ.
.TP
.B Detection
Linux uses the
.B cn_proc
netlink connector \(em event\-driven, sub\-second, idle between launches. Windows
has no equivalent and enumerates processes on the reconcile timer instead, so
detection latency there is whatever
.B reconcile_interval_s
is set to; lower it well below the 30\-second default. Correctness is unaffected
either way, because reconciliation is level\-triggered rather than
delta\-maintained.
.TP
.B Unit control
Linux drives systemd by default. Windows has no default supervisor \(em set the
per\-unit
.B *_cmd
overrides
.RB ( sc.exe ,
WinSW, and so on).
.TP
.B VRAM
Per\-process VRAM is unavailable under Windows' WDDM driver model
.RB ( nvidia-smi
reports
.B [N/A]
for every process), so eviction gates on service state rather than on VRAM
draining, and per\-unit VRAM is absent from
.IR /status .
.TP
.B Control socket
The unix control socket is Linux\-only. On Windows the write routes are
served on the TCP port instead, restricted to loopback peers only.
.TP
.B Presence detection
Linux\-only (evdev). On Windows presence is always reported as unknown.
.PP
On any other platform \(em macOS, notably \(em the daemon
.B refuses to start
and exits non\-zero: there is no process\-enumeration backend, and an empty
process list would read as
.IR available ,
so the daemon would never evict and would restart tenants into a live game. The
crate still builds and unit\-tests there, and the client subcommands
.RB ( status ", " wait ", " watch ", " --check-config )
work against a daemon anywhere.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gpu-arbiter-config (5),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR nvidia-smi (1)
